'''Daras''' ([[Yuzi]]: 西國; [[Zhuyin]]: ㄒㄧ ㄍㄨㄛˊ; [[Pinyin]]: ''Xiguó''), officially the '''Socialist Republic of Daras''' ([[Yuzi]]: 西國社会主义共和国; [[Zhuyin]]: ㄒㄧ ㄍㄨㄛˊ ㄕㄜˋ ㄏㄨㄟˋ ㄓㄨˇ ㄧˋ ㄍㄨㄥˋ ㄏㄜˊ ㄍㄨㄛˊ; [[Pinyin]]: ''Xiguó Shèhuì Zhǔyì Gònghéguó''), is a unitary [[sovereign state]] in [[Fosia]]. It borders [[Louyan]] to the east, [[Mengmia]] to the south, [[Shuchamia]] to the southeast, [[Nokor]] and the [[Jade Sea]] to the southwest, and [[Galastan]] to the northwest.

Daras was the seat of the [[Darasian Empire]], which completed its southern expansion in 924 and, under the [[Chengzhi Dynasty]], fought the [[Jade Sea War]] against the [[Kingdom of Zong]] (c. 1300–1390). Rapid industrialisation in the late nineteenth century was followed by the [[Darasian Socialist Revolution]] (1904–1905), which overthrew the imperial monarchy and established the Socialist Republic of Daras.

In 2016, the Darasian economy was Adonia's 6th largest by purchasing power parity. A [[List of states with nuclear weapons|nuclear weapons state]] and [[regional power]], it has the [[List of countries by number of military and paramilitary personnel|largest standing army]] in the world and ranks fifth in military expenditure. Daras is a [[Marxism–Jianism|Marxist–Jianist]] [[one-party republic]], where the role of the vanguard [[Communist Party of Daras|Communist Party]] is enshrined in the [[Constitution of Daras]].

==Etymology==

Daras comes from the [[Vasari language|Vasari]] ''darashm'' combining the words در ''dar'' (gate, door) and یشم ''ashm'' (jade) meaning "the jade gateway". It was mesogeanized to ''Daras'' over time.

The official name of the modern state is the "Socialist Republic of Daras" ([[Yuzi|Jade]]: 西國社会主义共和国; [[Zhuyin]]: ㄒㄧ ㄍㄨㄛˊ ㄕㄜˋ ㄏㄨㄟˋ ㄓㄨˇ ㄧˋ ㄍㄨㄥˋ ㄏㄜˊ ㄍㄨㄛˊ; [[Pinyin]]: ''Xiguó Shèhuì Zhǔyì Gònghéguó''). The shorter form is "Daras" or in [[Darasian language|Darasian]] ''Xiguó'' ([[Yuzi|Jade]]: 西國; [[Zhuyin]]: ㄒㄧ ㄍㄨㄛˊ) from ''xi'' ("western") and ''guó'' ("state"), a term which developed under the [[Bian dynasty]] in reference to its royal demesne. It was then applied to the area around [[Xunyi]] during the [[Jade Empire]] before being used as an occasional synonym for the state under the [[Liang dynasty]].

==History==
{{Main|History of Daras}}

===Darasian Empire===
The [[Darasian Empire]] administered the eastern segment of the [[Southern Transfosian Trade Route]], the overland corridor that carried trade between the Jade Sea and the Mesogean coast. Darasian imperial expansion southward was completed in 924, when the [[Kingdom of Seorabeol]] capitulated to Darasian imperial forces as the last holdout of the southern campaigns.

===Chengzhi Dynasty and the Jade Sea War===
{{Main|Jade Sea War}}
In the late thirteenth century the [[Chengzhi Dynasty]] conquered [[Dai Ngu]], [[Nokor]] and the [[Balausia Peninsula]]. The conquests gave Daras access to the Jade Sea and control of the western terminus of the [[Northern Transfosian Route]], and closed the alternative branches of the Southern Transfosian Trade Route. Darasian military procurement agents purchased Transfosian horses in bulk, depleting the reserves of the cavalry-dependent states of the Jade Peninsula and raising prices across the region.

Around 1300 the Chengzhi emperor [[Chengzhi Taizhu]] invaded [[Bingzhou]], one of the [[Three Luoyan States]], which was in personal union with the [[Kingdom of Zong]]. The invasion began the [[Jade Sea War]], fought between Daras and Zong for roughly ninety years. The war passed through the Taizhu offensive, the Jade Sea campaigns of the reign of [[Chengzhi Wuzhao]], and a long stalemate under [[Chengzhi Lingdi]], whose failed offensives exhausted the imperial treasury, before the emperor [[Chengzhi Heping]] negotiated a settlement around 1390. Zong preserved Bingzhou and the independence of the eastern seaboard. During the war the [[Kingdom of Bakas|Bakas Kingdom]], the western custodian of the Southern Transfosian Trade Route, collapsed in 1318.

===Trade blockade and continental isolation===
The settlement of the Jade Sea War was followed by a Darasian trade blockade that excluded Zong and the eastern seaboard states from the western trade routes to Illypnia. In the early fifteenth century the [[Jade Sea Plague]], a pandemic that spread across Fosia with a mortality of ten to fifteen per cent, originated in Daras. Commercial and diplomatic contact between Daras and the eastern seaboard states remained suspended for nearly two centuries. During the [[Great Peasant Revolt (Zong)|Great Peasant Revolt]] in Zong (1572–1585), Baiduist militias acquired Darasian matchlock muskets through smuggling via Daras. The Baiduist usurper [[Foyuan]] opened diplomatic relations with Daras during his reign (c. 1585–1589), ending the continental isolation.

===Modern period===

====Industrialisation====
During the latter half of the nineteenth century the state apparatus expanded under the reign of Emperor [[Wenzong]] to coordinate industrial development, and by the 1870s and 1880s Daras had become the second-largest industrial producer in Fosia, behind the Kingdom of Zong. Production quotas, resource allocation and labour deployment were administered by a technocratic bureaucracy that prioritised output over worker welfare. Labour unions formed across the mining and manufacturing sectors, first as mutual aid societies and later as political organisations, and coordinated strike action from the 1880s onward. The political organiser [[Li Jian]] published his thesis on collective governance in 1888, arguing for a state administered by worker collectives rather than the technocratic apparatus; his theory was later codified as [[Marxism–Jianism]].

====Darasian Socialist Revolution====
{{Main|Darasian Socialist Revolution}}
Emperor [[Taizong]], who succeeded Wenzong, had been trained in the industrial core and began implementing reforms that alienated the industrial apparatus and his own brother, Prince [[Yongzhe]]. Taizong was assassinated on 1 January 1904, an event known as the [[Dark January]], which precipitated the [[Darasian Socialist Revolution]]. The [[Heavenly Emperor's People's Army]] proclaimed the assassinated emperor the Eternal Socialist Emperor, or [[Heavenly King]], and fought the preservationist faction loyal to Yongzhe. [[Louyan]] sent 18,000 troops in support of Yongzhe, the Kingdom of Zong imposed a naval blockade on major Darasian cities between March and June 1904, and [[Breisland]] and [[Volisania]] provided tactical support to the preservationists. The [[Shansi]] region remained neutral throughout the conflict.

After the [[Auxin Offensive]] of June 1904 established a front along the Bu River, revolutionary forces in the south defeated a preservationist regiment at the [[Battle of Huchen]] in September 1904. The [[Bei Bu Offensive]] of December 1904 broke the preservationist lines, and the defection of General Gao Zihua opened a new front against the capital. The [[Winter Siege of Luanjing]] (20 December 1904 – 11 January 1905) ended with the flight of Yongzhe to Louyan and the surrender of the remaining preservationist forces under Qi Zhang at Wensha. The brief [[Daras–Louyan War]] of 1905 compelled Louyan to return Yongzhe, who was executed in February 1905.

====Socialist Republic of Daras====
The surviving revolutionary leader [[Chen Lixian]] became the [[Liaison to the Heavenly King]], the head of government of the new [[Socialist Republic of Daras]], and the former imperial capital Luanjing was renamed [[Gongjing]]. The [[Kingdom of Seorabeol]], which had negotiated neutrality during the revolution, was incorporated into the republic as the autonomous region of [[Kwangju]] in 1906. In the same year the Burawi socialist leader [[Maana Kadar]] visited Daras at Chen Lixian's invitation.

====Zong blockade of Daras====
{{Main|Zong blockade of Daras}}
From 1907 to 1911 the Kingdom of Zong imposed a naval blockade that denied Darasian-flag shipping access to the Jade Sea. The blockade ended with a memorandum of understanding that restored Darasian transit through the Jade Peninsula and imposed limits on Darasian naval construction and passage fees until 1925. Darasian trade shifted toward South Sea routes and closer ties with Breisland, Mercia and Volisania.

